  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\u0394x=0.25,$ the observed local truncation error is $\u03c4(0.25)=0.50.$\n\n- With step size $\u0394x=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $\u03c4(0.10)=0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method.",
  "worked_solution": "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\u03c4(\u0394x)=O\\,((\u0394x)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,\u0394x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $\u0394x$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.50$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.50}{0.01}=50.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & \u224850 \\\\ p+1 & \u2248{log}_{2.5}\u2061(50) \\\\ p+1 & \u22484.269\u2026 \\\\ p & \u22483.269\u2026.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$",
